  "known_asymmetries": [
    {
      "case": "11 CONTROL a future non-record subject token must still pass",
      "direction": "sql passes, js refuses \u2014 UNSAFE DIRECTION",
      "reason": "SQL holds no copy of the capture key vocabulary (deliberately \u2014 that copy is what broke in #560), so it cannot tell an invented subject token from a future legitimate one and accepts both. JS recomputes the exact key and refuses. Closing this in SQL means reintroducing the vocabulary, which is the defect this design removed. RESIDUAL EXPOSURE: a direct RPC call could approve a row the dashboard would refuse, IF something ever wrote a key the capture job does not produce. 0 such rows exist (measured 2026-09-22: all 46 ZIP-scope subjects are `zip-scope` (28) or `absence` (18), against a control of 9 project-scope rows). Resolving it is a founder call: accept, or give SQL a narrow closed subject set and pay the drift risk knowingly."
    }
  ]
